Hi Nicholas,

As agreed yesterday, I=E2=80=99ve attached the June and July board-cost extra=
ct and the current work standard for Trafford T2 and Warrington W1.

Connor, the CSV is from our purchase lines for the two sites under the same p=
rice contract. The weights are kg purchased and the costs are net material sp=
end in pounds. It leaves out purchases under other contracts, so it cannot gi=
ve you all board bought for T2 or the amount of B420 that actually went throu=
gh the feeder.

For sizing the feeder option, I pulled these figures this morning, 6 August, =
from the Trafford T2 production issues and returns ledger. These are net kg o=
f B420 fed through that feeder across all box specifications. I=E2=80=99ve ta=
ken out returns and counted board that went back through the feeder only once.

Aug=E2=80=93Oct 2023: 952,000 kg
Nov 2023=E2=80=93Jan 2024: 1,067,000 kg
Feb=E2=80=93Apr 2024: 987,000 kg
May=E2=80=93Jul 2024: 1,012,000 kg
Twelve months to 31 Jul 2024: 4,018,000 kg

If T2 runs at much the same level next year, use 4,000,000 kg for the annual =
illustration, rounded to the nearest 100,000 kg. The July B420 runs to S3 spe=
cification you=E2=80=99ll compare are part of that wider stream.

Neha, please use MCP-OPS-STD-2024-014 Rev 3 when you speak to the shift leads=
. It=E2=80=99s the version in force from 1 August. It calls for the first thr=
ee sheets to be checked and approved at both changeover and restart after a j=
am, with the two loss weights entered separately.

Best,
Caroline

Caroline Wood
Production Director
Marston Carton Packaging Ltd

